<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yuan Dynasty</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yuan Dynasty was the Mongol-ruled dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, proclaimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> in 1271, which completed the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> in 1279. It made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, its capital, promoted trade across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> and patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. Weakened by factional conflict, floods, plague and rebellion, it was driven from China in 1368 by the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty.jpg" alt="Yuan Dynasty" width="320" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cattette · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuan_Dynasty_revised.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Yuan was a Mongol-led dynasty and a successor state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> after its division. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> in 1206, and under his son Ögedei the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed the Jin dynasty in 1234, conquering most of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Kublai, unable to read Chinese, had Han teachers from his early years and sought the counsel of Chinese Buddhist and Confucian advisers. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a> granted him control of the Mongol territories in China, he built schools for Confucian scholars, issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> and revived Chinese rituals.</p><p>In 1271 Kublai issued the Proclamation of the Dynastic Name, naming the dynasty Great Yuan and claiming succession to the Chinese dynasties from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-sovereigns-and-five-emperors">Three Sovereigns and Five Emperors</a> to the Tang. He placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> in the imperial records as official founder, with the temple name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-song">Taizu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The early years were unstable. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a>, a grandson of Ögedei, refused to submit, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> remained an obstacle in the south. Kublai installed the hostage prince Wonjong as ruler of Goryeo in 1259, making it a tributary, and suppressed the revolt of Li Tan in 1262. The conquest was complete in 1279, when the Southern Song fell at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamen">Battle of Yamen</a>.</p><p>Succession was a lasting problem. Kublai's eldest son Zhenjin died in 1285, and Zhenjin's third son ruled as Temür Khan from 1294 to 1307. Elective <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultais</a> were held until 1311, but after 1320 they were a formality, and real power over succession lay with empress-dowagers, guard units or high officials. The final years brought struggle, famine and bitterness among the populace. In 1368 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> defeated the Yuan forces, and the Genghisid rulers retreated to the Mongolian Plateau, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a> lasted until 1635.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The population was divided into four classes: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semu">Semu</a> from western and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, the Han, mostly former subjects of the Jin, and the Nan or Southerners, subjects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a>, who stood at the bottom of the privilege ladder. The imperial examinations were not restarted until 1315. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> became the de facto state religion, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phags-pa-script">ʼPhags-pa script</a>, invented in 1269, was used to write Mongolian, Tibetan and Chinese. Underglaze blue and white <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> began at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingdezhen">Jingdezhen</a> in the early 14th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yuan dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Kaidu's Ögedeid domains and the Chagatai khan Duwa (r. 1282–1307), whom Kaidu had installed, then waged sustained war against the <b>Yuan dynasty</b> of Kublai Khan and his successors in Central Asia, raiding the Uyghur lands and Mongolia and keeping pressure on the Yuan frontier for nearly three decades.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dzungars were the leading tribe of the Oirats, the western Mongols, who had formed a loose confederation known as the Four Oirat after the fall of the <b>Yuan dynasty</b> and in the fifteenth century, under Esen Taishi, briefly dominated Mongolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its move of the capital to Zhongdu began Beijing's history as a main imperial capital, a role continued by the <b>Mongol Yuan</b>, the Ming and the Qing; the Lugou Bridge, known in the West as the Marco Polo Bridge, was built near Zhongdu between 1189 and 1192.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Mangrai also faced the Mongol <b>Yuan dynasty</b>, which had overrun the Dali kingdom and attacked Pagan; Yuan expeditions sent against Lan Na in the first years of the fourteenth century achieved little, and the kingdom later sent tribute missions to China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its ports linked south India with the Persian Gulf, the Arab world and <b>Yuan China</b>, and foreign visitors recorded the wealth of its pearl and horse trades: the accounts of Marco Polo and Wassaf are among the most detailed medieval descriptions of a south Indian kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Liao was the first of the so-called conquest dynasties, and its combination of steppe and Chinese government influenced the Jin dynasty of the Jurchen, the <b>Mongol Yuan</b> and, through them, the Manchu Qing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
